{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 2, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering approach to reducing penalties for drug possession will end this Sunday after a new measure reinstates criminal charges for holding small quantities of controlled substances. Under the updated HB4002 law, individuals found with illegal drugs such as fentanyl, heroin, or methamphetamine now face either prosecution or an alternative path that includes mandatory behavioral health programs and participation in a diversion initiative instead of fines. Possession for personal use remains classified as a misdemeanor, carrying a potential jail term of up to half a year. The legislation also strengthens enforcement against open-air drug use in public areas, while introducing tougher consequences for sales near spaces like playgrounds.\n\nSupporters of the new law argue it empowers local governments to establish customized treatment options, allowing them to tailor responses according to their financial capacities. Critics, however, contend the approach could result in inconsistent enforcement and differing outcomes across regions. Portland’s police chief, Bob Day, highlighted the strict conditions for deflection, excluding those with outstanding warrants, violent histories, or medical instability. Officers must quickly verify eligibility by contacting a county hotline, then await a mobile outreach team’s arrival within 30 minutes—failure to appear promptly could lead to immediate arrest.\n\nThe system relies on behavioral health responders to engage with individuals in the field, though delays may override this process. Day emphasized the urgency: if assistance doesn’t arrive on time, detainment proceeds without further delay. This shift marks a return to punitive measures, reversing Oregon’s earlier step toward reducing drug-related incarceration.\